le":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.315,"accessibilityScore":0.043,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[{"id":"character_driven","score":0.46,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["catalog text contains \"family\"","catalog text contains \"families\"","catalog text contains \"mother\""]},{"id":"biographical","score":0.368,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"family\"","catalog text contains \"families\"","catalog text contains \"mother\""]},{"id":"narrative","score":0.36,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"story of\"","catalog text contains \"reconstructs\""]},{"id":"synthesis","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"from\"","catalog text contains \"history of\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"The book argues that slave life differed between Mesopotamia and Mount Airy mainly because of demographic patterns shaping family formation, with Jamaica showing shrinking populations and Virginia showing expanding ones.","confidence":0.62},"academicOrientation":{"score":45,"confidence":0.35},"profileConfidence":0.66,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Jamaica","confidence":0.55},{"name":"Virginia","confidence":0.52},{"name":"Mesopotamia sugar estate","confidence":0.38}]},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"3e785144c486298eed0cd8578e7be726f7c812b3ef2b5fa495a4425485d1c0f5","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"\"This book reconstructs the individual lives and collective experiences of some 2,000 slaves on two plantations--Mesopotamia sugar estate in western Jamaica and Mount Airy Plantation in tidewater Virginia--during the final three generations of slavery in Jamaica and the USA. It also compares Mesopotamia with Mount Airy to demonstrate the differences between slave life in the British West Indies and slave life in the Antebellum US South. The chief difference was demographic. Mesopotamia had a continually shrinking slave population, with many more deaths than births, which was standard throughout the British Caribbean. Mount Airy had a continually expanding slave population, with many more births than deaths, which was standard throughout the Old South. At Mesopotamia the slaveholders imported their laborers from Africa, worked them to death and replaced them with new Africans, so that family life was perpetually stunted. At Mount Airy, where the slaves were all American-born, the slaveholders sold their surplus people or moved them to distant work sites, so that families were routinely broken up. On both plantations numerous individual slaves are observed in action, a mix of leaders and followers, rebels and conformists. A principal theme is slave motherhood and intergenerational family formation; another is the impact of field labor upon health and longevity.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Social conditions","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-tale-of-two-plantations-slave-life-and-labor-in-jamaica-and-virginia-richard-s-dunn"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Health and hygiene","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-tale-of-two-plantations-slave-life-and-labor-in-jamaica-and-virginia-richard-s-dunn"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Plantation life","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-tale-of-two-plantations-slave-life-and-labor-in-jamaica-and-virginia-richard-s-dunn"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Slaves","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-tale-of-two-plantations-slave-life-and-labor-in-jamaica-and-virginia-richard-s-dunn"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"History","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-a-tale-of-two-plantations-slave-life-and-labor-